    {
      "id": "manger-verbes-ger",
      "title": "Le verbe manger et les verbes en -ger",
      "minutes": 5,
      "lede": "Manger is a regular -er verb with one spelling trick: the nous form keeps an e so that the g stays soft.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Manger au présent",
          "headers": [
            "Sujet",
            "manger"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "je",
              "mange"
            ],
            [
              "tu",
              "manges"
            ],
            [
              "il / elle / on",
              "mange"
            ],
            [
              "nous",
              "mangeons"
            ],
            [
              "vous",
              "mangez"
            ],
            [
              "ils / elles",
              "mangent"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- Why the extra e? Before o and a, a bare g would sound hard (like “go”). The e keeps it soft [ʒ]: nous mangeons, not “nous mangons”. Same pattern for voyager, partager, ranger, nager, changer, déranger.\n- Verbs in -cer do the same trick with c → ç: commencer → nous commençons (the ç keeps the soft [s] sound). Only the nous form is affected.\n- Je mange, tu manges, il mange and ils mangent all sound the same [mɑ̃ʒ]. The -s and -ent are silent.\n- Manger has no preposition before a food: Je mange du riz (partitive), Je mange une pomme.\n- The imperative nous form keeps the e too: Mangeons ! (let's eat)."
        },
        {
          "type": "phrases",
          "title": "Écoutez",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je mange du riz à midi."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Tu manges souvent des légumes ?"
            },
            {
