    {
      "id": "son-j",
      "title": "Le son [j]",
      "minutes": 4,
      "lede": "The sound [j] is like the English y in “yes”. It is spelled in many ways: y, ï, i + vowel, and -il, -ill, -ail, -eil, -euil, -ouil.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Comment on l'écrit",
          "headers": [
            "Écriture",
            "Exemples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "y",
              "un yaourt · le yoga"
            ],
            [
              "ï",
              "l'aïe ! (ouch)"
            ],
            [
              "ie, io",
              "un ingrédient · délicieux"
            ],
            [
              "-ail",
              "l'ail · le travail"
            ],
            [
              "-eil, -euil",
              "une feuille · le soleil"
            ],
            [
              "-ouille",
              "la ratatouille · la grenouille"
            ],
            [
              "-ille",
              "la cuillère · la fille"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "Listen out for it",
          "text": "- -il or -ail at the end of a word is [j]: travail [tʁavaj], ail [aj]. The l is not pronounced.\n- -ille is usually [j] too: fille [fij], famille [famij], cuillère [kɥijɛʁ].\n- Exceptions: ville [vil], mille [mil], tranquille [tʁɑ̃kil] and Lille are pronounced with [l], not [j].\n- Smile and keep the tongue tip low behind the lower teeth; do not start a “dg” sound as in “jelly”."
        },
